The Epoch Capabilities index suggests that the open weight frontier lags behind the closed frontier [[https://epoch.ai/data-insights/open-closed-eci-gap][by about 4 months]]: so understanding the November State-of-the-Art probably becomes tractable starting in early 2026[fn:3]. 

[[../images/from_clipboard/20261008_143236.png]]

In [[https://planetbanatt.net/articles/agentic.html][Understanding Agentic LLMs]] we left off around GPT-5 / GLM-4.6's release, around late September 2025. We were just starting to see the reasoning + coding agent paradigm start to take shape. It seemed like we were starting to see glimpses of a future where [[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=87DyyMV0kCY][highly persistent models could spend a very long time on a task]]. What happened between then and now? 

** TODO DeepSeekMath-V2

Perhaps an appropriate place to start answering a question like "how did we go to average-ish coders to solving Navier-Stokes in under a year?" is DeepSeekMath-V2. This paper can be found [[https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22570][here]]. It came out on November 27, 2025.

Recall from DeepSeekMath that this was the original paper which introduced Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). That paper was mostly a reinforcement learning paper using math as a verifiable domain. In contrast, this paper outlines DeepSeek's path towards training a model which scored a gold medal on IMO 2025. [[https://x.com/OpenAI/status/1946594928945148246][OpenAI]] and [[https://deepmind.google/blog/advanced-version-of-gemini-with-deep-think-officially-achieves-gold-medal-standard-at-the-international-mathematical-olympiad/][DeepMind]] both achieved this in July 2025, which tracks roughly with our 4 month lag figure[fn:4]. 

Deepseek's code contribution in this paper is that to tackle sufficiently difficult problems, we need to be able to self-verify. We know we can use a verifiable outcome as the reward to encourage higher final-answer-accuracy, and we know we can scale test-time compute to spend more time on the problem. But if we don't have a way to demonstrate that we have correct reasoning on the way to the final answer, we may just get stuck spinning on useless tokens forever. How can we do this?

** TODO Kimi K2.5

Moonshot's Kimi line has some interesting ideas that make their approach somewhat different from the other Chinese labs. In particular, their 2.5 technical report offers two primary contributions: first, native multimodal training; second, RL training for multi-agent swarms. This paper can be found [[https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02276][here]]. It came out on February 2, 2026.

Native multimodal training is the de facto standard for frontier language models, in late 2026. Nearly all the frontier models are able to read images now, and models released without this ability are frequently criticized as lagging behind. It's not straightforward to make this work: we saw [[https://planetbanatt.net/articles/deepseek.html#orgb50a09e][in DeepSeek-VL]] that it's easy to degrade language capability when introducing vision capability. It seems like this was solved by the frontier at some point: how might that work?

Likewise, parallel agents are one paradigm we [[https://planetbanatt.net/articles/agentic.html#org4b2d990][briefly hinted at]] when we talked about Tongyi WebResearcher. There's two main ways you can manage context: you can either summarize the trajectory and compact the context, or you can delegate tasks to another disposable agent with an independent context. Kimi K2.5 is the first general-purpose open model to successfully train with this in mind. What might this unlock? How does that work?

** TODO GLM 5 -> 5.2 -> 5.3

Z.ai emerged as a true powerhouse in 2026, so much so that Anthropic released [[https://www.anthropic.com/research/glm-5-3-and-the-spread-of-advanced-cyber-capabilities][a blogpost about GLM-5.3]] being too capable of enabling cybercrime[fn:5]. 

GLM, in my view, can be viewed as the lab which is attempting to reach the frontier in the most "conventional" way compared to e.g. DeepSeek, Moonshot, MiniMax, etc. They got good at a few very specific things: 1) training on Huawei Ascend chips, 2) On-Policy Distillation, 3) scaling up.

*** TODO GLM-5

The original GLM-5 paper can be found [[https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15763][here]]. It came out on February 17, 2026. Per Artificial Analysis, GLM-5.3 was just about the closest open model to Opus 4.5's capabilities, freely available for download on huggingface. 

[[../images/from_clipboard/20261008_161354.png]]

GLM 5 largely follows DeepSeek-v3.2. It uses DeepSeek Sparse Attention (DSA) with Multi-latent Attention (MLA), multi-token prediction, and so on. It makes some minor changes to these (e.g. Muon Split, a modification of MLA to make it more compatable with the Muon optimizer) but for the most part builds on existing approaches.

Where GLM-5 differs is mostly in infra and post-training. Probably most importantly, GLM-5 is natively trained on Huawei Ascend chips, which made them far less compute constrained compared to other labs relying on Nvidia GPUs. This will come in handy when we later talk about [[https://z.ai/blog/glm-5.3-flash][GLM-5.3-flash]], where they were able to serve an absolutely mind-boggling amount of tokens in a short time.

On post-training, GLM-5 was able to maximize GPU utilization during post-training by making this step more asynchronous. This is partially related to their excellent on-policy distillation, which they use to do several post-training steps concurrently as different expert models, and then merge at the end.

[[../images/from_clipboard/20261008_172951.png]]

*** TODO GLM-5.2

GLM-5.2 was an update to the GLM-5 line which extended GLM-5 to 1M context, multiple effort levels, and some minor architecture improvements targetting speed. This post can be found [[https://z.ai/blog/glm-5.2][here]]. It came out on June 16, 2026.

GLM-5.2 scales up their /Expert Models/ approach from GLM-4.5. Recall [[https://planetbanatt.net/articles/agentic.html#orgd78d255][from that work]] that they make three finetunes of the original model for reasoning, agent work, and general chat, and then do self-distillation to combine them all together at the end. GLM-5.2 claimed to train ten of these models, and used [[https://github.com/THUDM/slime][slime]] to efficiently distill these 10 models into the final model[fn:6]. 

