[fn:6] Zhipu is amazing at distillation. I think this is not properly appreciated as a direct cause for their success, a genuine technical achievement rather than just something usable as a cheap dunk. [[https://www.anthropic.com/threat-intelligence-report-september-2026][Anthropic]] has revealed some fairly extensive distillation practices by Zhipu in order to copy Claude's outputs.

I think conversation about the distillation campaigns are often mired with discussions about whether this should be allowed, or whether it's happening at all, etc. I think frequently missing from this conversation is that distillation is a really important capability for a lab to have, e.g. for merging expert models together, for training smaller models like Gemini Flash or Gemma, etc. [[https://thinkingmachines.ai/news/introducing-inkling/][Thinking Machines's Inkling]] is a good barometer for me: a western lab which is pretty open about how it's attempting to distill Kimi K2.5, and then in turn used to train a [[https://thinkingmachines.ai/news/inkling-small/][smaller version]] via on-policy distillation.

A minor rant, maybe, but it does annoy me to see people talk about distillation as a means to discredit the technical ability of the researchers at Chinese labs. All serious labs do extensive work on distillation, even if they never (have to) use it upon any model outside their own doors. Weaker western labs are behind the open-weight Chinese models [[https://www.forbes.com/sites/antoniopequenoiv/2026/04/30/elon-musk-admits-xai-distilled-openai-data-to-train-models-heres-what-that-means/][because they are worse at it]], not because they're nobly foregoing it. 

